## Materials and methods

### Sample preparation

The MgO layer was deposited by RF sputtering at room temperature, while W, Ta, Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn, and Ru layers were deposited by DC sputtering at 400 \({}^{\circ}\)C on a heated stage. The deposited stack was annealed at 500 \({}^{\circ}\)C for an hour in a vacuum. The buffer layer W(2 nm)/Ta(3 nm) is for epitaxial growth of the M-plane oriented hexagonal \(D0_{19}\)-Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn [22, 39]. The structural properties of our sample are similar to those in our previous study [7]. The stack was processed into a Hall device using electron beam lithography and Ar ion milling. To protect the channel layer, we deposited Ta(3 nm)/Ru(1 nm) films after the fabrication of the Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn nanodot. The coplanar waveguide and contact pads made of Cr(5 nm)/Au(100 nm) electrodes were formed after fabricating the channel.

### DC measurements

In the DC-regime measurements (Fig. **1c** and **d**), a current source (Yokogawa; Model GS200) was used for electric current application into the channel. The Hall voltage was measured using a voltmeter (Keithley; Model 2182A) with a DC current of 0.1 mA, corresponding to \(J_{\rm HM}=4.26\) MA cm-2. The shunt current was estimated by a method we reported in our previous study [7]. The \(R_{\rm H}\) - \(H_{\rm Z}\) hysteresis curve was obtained by sweeping \(H_{\rm Z}\) at a rate of 10 mT/s. For the \(R_{\rm H}\)-\(J_{\rm HM}\) measurement, we injected a 100 ms-pulse from the current source.

### Switching probability measurement

In the switching probability measurements (Fig. **2a-e** and Fig. **5a-c**), pulse and DC voltages were applied into the channel from a function generator (Keysight; Model 81160). The Hall voltage was measured by a voltmeter (Keithley; Model 2182A) in the presence of a DC voltage of 30 mV, corresponding to \(J_{\rm HM}=3.42\) MA cm-2. The transmitted waveforms of the applied pulse and DC voltages were monitored using an oscilloscope (Agilent; Model DSOX93204A) connected to the output side of the waveguide. Supplementary Fig. 1 shows the measured waveforms of the applied voltage (\(V_{\rm OSC}\)) for four different \(\tau_{P}\). The amplitude of the pulse current is determined by \(V_{\rm OSC}/R_{\rm OSC}\), where \(R_{\rm OSC}\)\((=50\)\(\Omega)\) is the internal impedance of the oscilloscope.

The procedure of the switching-probability measurement is schematically shown in Supplementary Fig. **2a**. First, the octupole state in the Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn nanodot was initialized to a low \(V_{\rm H}\) state by a pulse with \(J_{\rm HM}=-55\) MA cm-2 and \(\tau_{P}=5\) ms, under an in-plane magnetic field. The histogram of the measured \(V_{\rm H}\) after the initialization is displayed in Supplementary Fig. **2b**, which confirms the successful initialization with no writing error. Next, the writing pulse was applied and then \(V_{\rm H}\) was measured. This whole sequence was repeated 60 times to evaluate \(P\) for given \((J_{\rm HM},\tau_{P})\). Supplementary Figs. **2c-f** show the distributions of \(V_{\rm H}\) against \(J_{\rm HM}\) for \(\tau_{\rm P}=5\) ns, 500 ns, 50 \(\mu\)s and 5 ms, respectively, with \(\mu_{0}H_{x}=200\) mT. The gap between the high and low values of \(V_{\rm H}\) is sufficiently large for an unambiguous evaluation of \(P\).

## Numerical simulation

We model our Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn nanodot by the following magnetic energy density

\[u=J_{0}\sum_{i,j}\mathbf{m}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{m}_{j}-D_{0}\mathbf{e}_{y}\cdot \sum_{i,j}\mathbf{m}_{i}\times\mathbf{m}_{j}-K\sum_{i=A,B,C}\big{(}\mathbf{m}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{e}_ {K,i}\big{)}^{2}\] \[-\frac{K_{\rm z}}{3}\sum_{i=A,B,C}\big{(}\mathbf{m}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{Kz, i}\big{)}^{2}-\ M_{\rm s}\mathbf{H}\cdot\sum_{i=A,B,C}\mathbf{m}_{i} \tag{3}\]

where \(J_{0}\) is the antiferromagnetic exchange coupling constant, \(D_{0}\) is the Dzyaloshinskii-Moriya coupling constant, \(K\) is the uniaxial anisotropy constant originating from the local crystalline symmetry, \(K_{\rm z}\) represents the global uniaxial anisotropy induced predominantly by the interface, and \(\mathbf{H}\) is the external magnetic field. The unit vectors \(\mathbf{e}_{K,i}\) and \(\mathbf{e}_{Kz,i}\), representing the easy axes of the anisotropies, are given by \(\mathbf{e}_{K,\rm A}=-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{e}_{x}+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\mathbf{e}_{z}\), \(\mathbf{e}_{K,\rm B}=-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{e}_{x}-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\mathbf{e}_{z}\), \(\mathbf{e}_{K,\rm C}=\mathbf{e}_{x}\), \(\mathbf{e}_{Kz,\rm A}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\mathbf{e}_{x}-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{e}_{z}\), \(\mathbf{e}_{Kz,\rm B}=-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\mathbf{e}_{x}-\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{e}_{z}\), and \(\mathbf{e}_{Kz,\rm C}=\mathbf{e}_{z}\). Here we set the \(x\), \(y\), and \(z\) axes along \([11\bar{2}0]\), \([0001]\) and \([1\bar{1}00]\), respectively, of Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn.

The coupled LLG equations for the sublattice magnetizations are given by

\[\frac{\partial\mathbf{m}_{i}}{\partial t}=-\gamma\mathbf{m}_{i}\times\big{(}\,\mathbf{H}_ {\rm eff}^{i}+\ \mathbf{H}_{\rm th}^{i}\big{)}+\alpha\mathbf{m}_{i}\times\frac{\partial\mathbf{m}_{i}}{ \partial t}-\gamma D_{\rm s}J_{\rm HM}\mathbf{m}_{i}\times(\mathbf{m}_{i}\times\mathbf{s}) \tag{4}\]

where \(\mathbf{H}_{\rm eff}^{i}=-\frac{1}{M_{\rm s}}\frac{\partial u}{\partial\mathbf{m}_{i}}\) is the effective magnetic field exerted on \(\mathbf{m}_{i}\), and \(\mathbf{H}_{\rm th}^{i}\) describes thermal fluctuations satisfying \(\langle\,H_{\rm th}^{i,\mu}(t)\rangle=0\) and \(\langle\,H_{\rm th}^{i,\mu}(t)\,H_{\rm th}^{i,\nu}(t^{\prime})\rangle=\frac{2 \alpha k_{\rm B}T}{\gamma M_{\rm s}\nu}\delta_{\mu\nu}\delta(t-t^{\prime})\), with \(\mu,\nu=x,y,z\), \(k_{\rm B}\) the Boltzmann constant, \(V=\frac{\pi D^{2}d}{4}\) the volume of the Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn nanodot, and \(D\) and \(d\) the diameter and thickness of the nanodot, respectively. The last term in Eq. (4) represents the SOT with \(D_{\rm s}=\frac{1}{3}\frac{\hbar\theta_{\rm SH}}{2eM_{\rm s}d}\) anddenoting the effective spin Hall angle. The factor 1/3 in \(D_{\rm S}\) reflects the sublattice symmetry where the spin current injected into Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn is transferred equiprobably to each of the three magnetic sublattices.

The antiferromagnetic order parameters for a noncollinear AFM can be constructed as[28, 30]

\[\mathbf{n}_{1}=\frac{\mathbf{m}_{\rm A}+\mathbf{m}_{\rm B}-2\mathbf{m}_{\rm C}}{3 \sqrt{2}},\qquad\mathbf{n}_{2}=\frac{-\mathbf{m}_{\rm A}+\mathbf{m}_{\rm B}}{\sqrt{6}}. \tag{5}\]

Assuming the net magnetization to be sufficiently small as \(|\mathbf{m}|=\frac{|\mathbf{m}_{\rm A}+\mathbf{m}_{\rm B}+\mathbf{m}_{\rm C}|}{3}\ll\,1\), it is straightforward to show \(\mathbf{n}_{1}\cdot\mathbf{n}_{2}\simeq 0\) and \(|\mathbf{n}_{1}|\simeq|\mathbf{n}_{2}|\simeq 1/\sqrt{2}\). Therefore, tracking either one of \(\mathbf{n}_{1}\) or \(\mathbf{n}_{2}\) is sufficient to capture the dynamics of the noncollinear AFM order in Mn\({}_{3}\)Sn. \(\mathbf{n}_{1}\) can be interpreted as corresponding to the octupole moment, and in the main text, we adopted the notation \(\mathbf{n}\equiv\mathbf{n}_{1}\).

For the simulations discussed in the main text, we employed the following parameters: \(J_{0}=8\times 10^{7}\)\(\mathrm{Jm}^{-3}\), \(D_{0}=6\times 10^{6}\)\(\mathrm{Jm}^{-3}\), \(K=2.5\times 10^{5}\)\(\mathrm{Jm}^{-3}\), \(K_{\rm z}=9\times 10^{2}\)\(\mathrm{Jm}^{-3}\), \(M_{\rm S}=0.5\)\(\mathrm{T}\), \(\gamma=2.21\times 10^{5}\)\(\mathrm{mA}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), \(\alpha=0.005\), \(\theta_{\rm SH}=-0.1\), \(d=20\) nm, \(D=200\) nm, and \(T=300\) K.

### Theoretical analysis

Our derivation of Eq. (1) in the main text follows Ref. [30]. Here we outline the derivation briefly. We first rewrite the LLG equations (4) in terms of \((\mathbf{n}_{1},\mathbf{n}_{2},\mathbf{m})\), and introduce the angular variable by \(\mathbf{n}_{1}=\frac{\mathbf{1}}{\sqrt{2}}\left(\sin\theta\,,0,\cos\theta\right)\). Because of the conditions \(\mathbf{n}_{1}\cdot\mathbf{n}_{2}\simeq 0\) and \(D_{0}>0\), one finds \(\mathbf{n}_{2}=\frac{\mathbf{1}}{\sqrt{2}}\left[\sin\left(\theta+\frac{\pi}{2}\right),0,\cos\left(\theta+\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\right]\). Taking advantage of the condition \(|\mathbf{m}|\ll 1\), a perturbative treatment of \(\mathbf{m}\) up to its first order leads to an expression of \(\mathbf{m}\) as a function of \(\mathbf{n}_{1}\)[28, 30]. Putting all these together, we arrive at the equation of motion for \(\theta\), that is Eq. (1), where \(H_{\text{E}}=\frac{3J_{0}}{M_{\text{s}}}\) and \(H_{K}=\frac{2K_{\text{Z}}}{3M_{\text{s}}}\). The uncompensated net magnetization is given by \(M_{\text{UC}}=\frac{M_{\text{s}}K}{J_{0}}\) in equilibrium without an external field.

\(J_{\text{C1}}^{*}\) and \(J_{\text{C2}}^{*}\) are derived from Eq. (2) as follows. A critical current density and the corresponding critical angle \(\theta^{*}\) are defined as \((J_{\text{C}},\theta)\) that simultaneously satisfy \(\partial_{\theta}u=0\) and \(\partial_{\theta}^{2}u=0\). Assuming that \(|H_{x}|\) is sufficiently small that it hardly affects \(\theta^{*}\), we find \(\theta^{*}\cong\frac{\pi}{4},\frac{3\pi}{4},\frac{5\pi}{4},\frac{7\pi}{4}\). The critical angle corresponding to the down-to-up transition, with the initial state \(\theta=\pi\), is therefore \(\frac{5\pi}{4}\). Substituting \(\theta=\frac{5\pi}{4}\) to \(\partial_{\theta}u=0\), we reach the expression of \(J_{\text{C1}}^{*}\) given in the main text. \(J_{\text{C2}}^{*}\) can be obtained in a similar fashion considering the up-to-down transition.

